'''Lucumí Tarica''' ({{Langx|mxi|لُوكُومِي تَارِيكَا|Lukúmí Taríka}}; {{Langx|mao|Lucumí Ona|4=path of friends}}), alternatively known as '''Camin de Orixá-Djinn''' ({{Literal translation|way of the {{W|Orisha}}-{{W|Jinn}}}}) or simply '''Lucumí''', is a [[W:Syncretic religion|syncretic]] [[W:African diasporic religion|Kaftian diasporic religion]] originating and primarily practiced in [[Balisca]] and [[Asikyira]]. It emerged from the complex interaction between traditional [[W:African traditional religions|Kaftian traditional religions]] (particularly the [[W:Yoruba religion|Imo]] spiritual system), [[W:Islam|Islam]], and, to a lesser extent, [[W:Christianity|Christianity]], from the 16th century onwards. Lucumí Tarica uniquely centers upon the concepts of the [[W:Orisha|Orisha]] (divine forces and deified ancestors in Imo cosmology) and the [[W:Jinn|Jinn]] (intelligent, unseen beings of [[W:Islamic mythology|Islamic mythology]]), defining itself as a spiritual journey or way of life for the Lucumí community.

